  "narrative": "All new feature_023 claims have direction -1, so positive signed estimates indicate stronger dependency in feature_023=0 versus feature_023=1. The new cross-outcome audit does not support a broad feature_023 association. H121 (outcome_015) is positive but only +0.0075, below its approximate 0.0250 clinical threshold, with an interval including null and values below threshold; I reject it despite the upper bound crossing the threshold because the point estimate is small and the broader feature_023 screen is incoherent. H122-H128 are mostly opposite or null: outcome_016 -0.0015, outcome_012 -0.0046, outcome_013 -0.0140, outcome_002 -0.0039, outcome_005 -0.0004, outcome_008 -0.0106, and outcome_011 -0.0218, all with intervals spanning null and none providing a positive threshold-aligned signal. Therefore I reject and close H122-H128, reject H3, and defer the remaining low-benchmark feature_023 candidates H118 and H119 because their direct low-benchmark signals are not sufficiently coherent across outcomes or decisively validated to accept.\n\nThe reversed feature_027 screens produce a much stronger but suspicious pattern. For direction +1, positive estimates mean feature_027=1 is associated with weaker dependency. H130 on outcome_008 is opposite and imprecise (-0.0157; interval -0.0437 to +0.0123), with its upper bound below the outcome_008 threshold of about 0.0282, so it is rejected and closed; this argues against a broad cross-outcome weaker-dependency effect. In contrast, H131 and H132 test outcome_014 feature_027=1 versus 0 within feature_034=1 and feature_034=0. Both have enormous positive estimates, +0.7886 and +0.8042, with tight intervals entirely above zero and far above the outcome_014 threshold of about 0.0093. They mirror the overall H120 discovery estimate (+0.796) and show that the effect is not confined to one feature_034 subgroup. This is strong discovery evidence for a very large outcome_014-specific weaker-dependency association. However, because the effect is extreme relative to the outcome scale, was prompted by an initially rejected opposite-direction screen, and could reflect coding, distributional, or subgroup-coding artifacts, I do not accept H131/H132 or H120 yet. I request independent validation of the overall H120 claim to test whether this unusually large association replicates outside the discovery sample before accepting the feature_027 conclusion. If validation is positive and threshold-aligned, I will reconsider accepting H120 and the subgroup claims; if it is null, opposite, or overly unstable, I will close the family as a dataset-specific anomaly. All statements remain associations in this dataset, not causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 10 appraise update. Accepted set remains empty. Feature_023 coherence screen failed: reject/close H121-H128; reject/close H3. H118 and H119 are low-benchmark feature_023 candidates but now deferred, not accepted, because independent/interval uncertainty and poor cross-outcome coherence remain. H118 has positive validation but very wide interval; H119 has positive discovery but no validation and broader family incoherence.\n\nFeature_027 reversed family: H130 outcome_008 opposite/null, reject/close, so broad weaker-dependency effect is unsupported. H131/H132 outcome_014 feature_034 subgroup tests are enormous, tight, and mutually consistent: +0.7886 (feature_034=1) and +0.8042 (feature_034=0), far exceeding outcome_014 threshold ~0.0093. These support H120 but are extreme and potentially artifact-like, so H131/H132 remain unresolved active pending validation of overall H120. Validation requested: H120. Next synthesize: assess H120 independent validation; if it is positive and threshold-aligned, consider accepting H120 and then H131/H132 or a consolidated H120 claim; if null/opposite/wide, reject or close H120 and likely close H131/H132. No causal or generalizable interpretation.",
  "validate": "H120"
}

