  "narrative": "The independent validation resolves the main current candidate. For H120, the signed hypothesis is that feature_027=1 versus feature_027=0 has a less negative outcome_014 CRISPR dependency score, i.e. weaker dependency. Discovery was +0.7961, 95% CI +0.7759 to +0.8163. Independent validation was +0.8065, multiplicity-adjusted CI +0.7754 to +0.8376. The 10% absolute-mean clinical benchmark for outcome_014 is about 0.0093, so both independent estimates are roughly 85 times that threshold and the intervals are entirely positive and far above the threshold. I therefore accept H120 as a dataset-specific association: within this supplied dataset, feature_027=1 is associated with dramatically weaker dependency for outcome_014 relative to feature_027=0. This is not a causal or generalizable claim; it is a replicated measured association in this dataset.\n\nThe response-deadline claims fail validation and are rejected. H107 had a strong discovery result for outcome_008 feature_055 level_003 versus level_001 within feature_002=1, but independent validation was opposite and null-centered (+0.0614 discovery versus -0.0124 validation; validation CI -0.0650 to +0.0402). H48 was null and validation was also null/opposite, so it is closed. H113, the opposite direction for feature_027 on outcome_014, is rejected because both discovery and validation favor the reversed weaker-dependency claim H120 rather than stronger dependency.\n\nThe feature_027 effect is outcome-specific rather than broad. H129 on outcome_006 was positive but below its approximate 0.0093 threshold, so I reject and close it. H130 on outcome_008 was opposite, and prior feature_027 tests were not threshold-aligned across other outcomes. H131 and H132 show the same enormous positive effect within both feature_034=1 and feature_034=0 subgroups, but the independent validation tested the overall H120 contrast rather than each subgroup separately. I therefore keep the subgroup claims unresolved and active, pending possible subgroup-level validation or new subgroup confirmation analyses.\n\nThe feature_023 family remains uncertain but is not accepted. H118 has positive discovery and validation estimates for outcome_006 feature_023=0 versus 1, but the adjusted validation interval is very wide and includes substantial null or opposite values. The cross-outcome feature_023 screen was mostly null or opposite, especially on higher-benchmark outcomes. H119, the corresponding outcome_014 feature_023 contrast, remains positive in discovery but is not independently validated. I keep H118 unresolved/deferred and H119 unresolved/active because H119 is the most direct next validation target that could either resolve or close this low-benchmark feature_023 signal. The main next priorities are validating H119 and running the proposed feature_002/feature_060 subgroup confirmations of the accepted feature_027 association to characterize its dataset-specific stability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 10 synthesis update. Accepted set now contains H120: outcome_014, feature_027=1 vs 0, direction +1, weaker dependency. Evidence: discovery +0.7961 CI +0.7759,+0.8163; validation +0.8065 adjusted CI +0.7754,+0.8376; outcome_014 10% threshold ~0.0093. Strong dataset-specific association, not causal/generalizable.\n\nRejected/closed: H107 due validation null/opposite; H48 null/opposite; H129 broad feature_027 on outcome_006 below threshold; H113 opposite direction rejected with validation support for H120. Feature_023 broad signal closed by H121-H128 incoherence. Feature_027 broad signal unsupported: outcome_006 small/below threshold, outcome_008 opposite, other tests not aligned.\n\nUnresolved: H118 outcome_006 feature_023 low-benchmark positive but wide validation; H119 outcome_014 feature_023 positive discovery, unvalidated; H131/H132 feature_034 subgroup tests for H120 are extremely large and mutually coherent but not independently validated as subgroups.\n\nNext priorities: appraise validation of H119 if budget permits and evidence still justifies; positive threshold-aligned validation could accept a second low-benchmark feature_023 association, null/opposite/wide would close feature_023. Also proposed confirmatory H120 subgroup mean differences within feature_002=1/0 and feature_060=1/0, plus interaction by feature_002=1, to characterize whether the accepted feature_027 effect is stable across common binary predictors or concentrated in a coding pattern. If H131/H132 later validated, they can be accepted; otherwise they remain descriptive subgroup evidence."
